David Ernst

Usage:
    convert-images [options] <inputDir> <outputDir>
Options:
    -h, --help      Print this help page.
    -c, --crop      crop excessive whitespace
    -a, --always    convert every image, including one whose file size says it is
                    already starved. Without it such an image is left alone: see
                    below.
    -r, --reverse   reverse: convert the -e format back to jpeg
    -e, --encoding <format>
                    output encoding: avif, webp or jxl, default avif
    -u, --chroma <chroma>
                    AVIF chroma: 420 or 444. 444 keeps colour at full resolution,
                    420 at a quarter of it; 444 gives sharper coloured lines and
                    larger files. Default 420. AVIF only.
    -j, --jobs <jobs>
                    Caps the number of encoders.
                    Default: what the cores allow, or what the RAM holds of
                    the largest image in the input, whichever is fewer.
    -l, --quality <quality>
                    quality level of the output images
    -s, --speed <speed>
                    av1 speed preset 0-9, lower is slower, default 5
    -m, --max-resolution <maxRes>
                    maximum resolution (height) to keep
    -f, --fuzz <fuzz>
                    when trimming, how many percent color difference gets still trimmed
    -k, --calibrate calibrate: measure what the GPU encoder has to be asked for to
                    match the CPU, on crops of the images in <inputDir>, and
                    save it. Works in <outputDir>; converts nothing.
    -v, --verbose   Also say why each item was handled the way it was, and on a
                    failure print the tool's whole output rather than its last
                    lines.
    -q, --quiet     Print errors only - no progress and no warnings.
    -x, --set <name=value>
                    Override a setting for this run: name=value, repeatable. The
                    value is held where the environment holds it, so it wins
                    over the settings file; a secret is refused - the settings
                    file is where it goes. The settings page lists the names.
